1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:06,565
In lesson two, a sliding window kept
the conversation small, but it forgot the

2
00:00:06,565 --> 00:00:07,437
user's name.

3
00:00:07,787 --> 00:00:13,034
In this lesson, instead of throwing
old turns away, you'll compress them into a

4
00:00:13,034 --> 00:00:14,495
short running summary.

5
00:00:14,845 --> 00:00:17,347
Small like a window,
but it keeps the gist.

6
00:00:19,147 --> 00:00:23,066
You'll explain summary memory,
and when it beats a plain window.

7
00:00:23,666 --> 00:00:27,383
Summarize old turns,
with one extra model call.

8
00:00:27,983 --> 00:00:30,888
Send the summary along
with the system prompt.

9
00:00:31,488 --> 00:00:35,176
And know the costs: lost
details, and an extra call.

10
00:00:36,976 --> 00:00:38,621
Think about a long meeting.

11
00:00:38,971 --> 00:00:42,137
Afterwards, nobody
re-reads the full transcript.

12
00:00:42,487 --> 00:00:47,123
People read the minutes: the decisions,
who agreed to what, and what's still open.

13
00:00:47,473 --> 00:00:50,830
Summary memory does the
same for a conversation.

14
00:00:51,180 --> 00:00:56,806
The old part becomes minutes, and only
the last few turns are kept word for word.

15
00:00:57,406 --> 00:01:00,147
Old turns become a short, running summary.

16
00:01:00,747 --> 00:01:05,164
Recent turns are kept exactly,
because details matter most there.

17
00:01:05,764 --> 00:01:10,528
And every request sends the summary,
the recent turns, and the new message.

18
00:01:11,128 --> 00:01:15,388
Running means the summary is
updated, not rewritten from scratch.

19
00:01:15,738 --> 00:01:20,744
Each time, the model merges the old
summary with the turns that are leaving.

20
00:01:21,094 --> 00:01:23,739
So it stays short
however long the chat gets.

21
00:01:25,539 --> 00:01:26,895
Here's how it works.

22
00:01:27,245 --> 00:01:29,337
After each turn, check the size.

23
00:01:29,687 --> 00:01:31,959
Is the conversation above our limit?

24
00:01:32,559 --> 00:01:38,511
If so, split it into old turns and the
last few turns, cutting where a turn starts,

25
00:01:38,511 --> 00:01:39,730
as in lesson two.

26
00:01:40,330 --> 00:01:42,109
Then make one extra call.

27
00:01:42,459 --> 00:01:47,494
Give the model the old summary and
the old turns, and ask for a new summary.

28
00:01:48,094 --> 00:01:51,714
Finally, keep the new
summary, and drop the old turns.

29
00:01:52,064 --> 00:01:54,656
But only after the summary has arrived.

30
00:01:55,006 --> 00:01:57,259
If the call fails, nothing is lost.

31
00:01:59,059 --> 00:02:00,252
Let's see it run.

32
00:02:00,602 --> 00:02:02,761
Same five turns as lesson two.

33
00:02:03,111 --> 00:02:06,093
The code is on the
left, the output on the right.

34
00:02:06,693 --> 00:02:12,739
First, the same conversation as last
lesson: five turns, about seven hundred and

35
00:02:12,739 --> 00:02:13,797
thirty tokens.

36
00:02:14,397 --> 00:02:20,287
The summarize function builds a prompt
with the summary so far, or none, and the old

37
00:02:20,287 --> 00:02:23,161
messages written out as plain text lines.

38
00:02:23,761 --> 00:02:29,050
Then one extra model call, with a
system prompt written just for summarizing.

39
00:02:29,650 --> 00:02:34,906
Compact folds the old turns in when
we're over the limit, keeping the last two

40
00:02:34,906 --> 00:02:35,310
turns.

41
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:41,564
Six messages folded, and the size drops
from about seven hundred and thirty tokens

42
00:02:41,564 --> 00:02:43,220
to about three hundred.

43
00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:44,953
Here's the summary.

44
00:02:45,303 --> 00:02:50,521
In offline mode the fake writes a
simple extract of what the learner said, and

45
00:02:50,521 --> 00:02:53,464
labels it, so we
never pretend it's a model.

46
00:02:53,814 --> 00:02:57,594
A real model writes a
proper summary in its own words.

47
00:02:58,194 --> 00:03:00,982
And now the question
again: what's my name?

48
00:03:01,332 --> 00:03:06,354
The summary goes into the system
prompt, and this time the answer is: Anuj.

49
00:03:06,704 --> 00:03:09,484
As small as the
window, but it kept the name.

50
00:03:11,284 --> 00:03:16,935
In the project, the summarizer's system
prompt says what to keep: facts about the

51
00:03:16,935 --> 00:03:21,051
learner, topics covered,
open questions, and promises made.

52
00:03:21,401 --> 00:03:25,075
And it sets a limit: at
most a hundred and twenty words.

53
00:03:25,425 --> 00:03:29,287
Without a limit, summaries
slowly grow back into transcripts.

54
00:03:31,087 --> 00:03:32,988
Here's compact in the project.

55
00:03:33,338 --> 00:03:37,121
Under the limit, or with too
few turns to fold, it does nothing.

56
00:03:37,721 --> 00:03:41,934
Otherwise it cuts where a turn
starts, so tool pairs stay together.

57
00:03:42,534 --> 00:03:43,812
And the order matters.

58
00:03:44,162 --> 00:03:45,392
Summarize first.

59
00:03:45,742 --> 00:03:50,490
Only when the new summary has
arrived do we drop the old messages.

60
00:03:50,840 --> 00:03:54,445
If the API call fails,
every message is still there.

61
00:03:56,245 --> 00:03:58,185
So which strategy should you use?

62
00:03:58,535 --> 00:04:02,820
Full history keeps everything
exactly, but grows every turn.

63
00:04:03,420 --> 00:04:07,753
A window keeps recent turns
exactly, but forgets older ones.

64
00:04:08,353 --> 00:04:14,606
A summary plus a window keeps the gist
of old turns and the recent turns exactly.

65
00:04:14,956 --> 00:04:19,675
It costs an extra call now and
then, and fine details can be lost.

66
00:04:20,275 --> 00:04:25,669
And for the facts that must never be
lost, like a name, we add long-term memory,

67
00:04:25,669 --> 00:04:26,478
next lesson.

68
00:04:28,278 --> 00:04:29,435
Pause and try.

69
00:04:29,785 --> 00:04:35,233
Here are some old turns from Riya: I'm
preparing for a Python interview on Friday.

70
00:04:35,583 --> 00:04:38,543
Lists are fine, decorators confuse me.

71
00:04:38,893 --> 00:04:40,519
Can you quiz me tomorrow?

72
00:04:41,119 --> 00:04:43,834
Write a summary in at
most twenty five words.

73
00:04:44,184 --> 00:04:45,442
What must it keep?

74
00:04:52,042 --> 00:04:55,725
One good summary: Riya,
Python interview on Friday.

75
00:04:56,075 --> 00:04:59,275
Comfortable with lists,
struggles with decorators.

76
00:04:59,625 --> 00:05:01,404
Promised: a quiz tomorrow.

77
00:05:01,754 --> 00:05:05,106
It keeps the fact, the goal,
the struggle, and the promise.

78
00:05:06,906 --> 00:05:08,048
Interview corner.

79
00:05:08,398 --> 00:05:11,396
How would you keep a long
running chat within budget?

80
00:05:13,996 --> 00:05:19,789
A good answer: keep the last few
turns word for word, fold older turns into a

81
00:05:19,789 --> 00:05:25,657
running summary with a dedicated
prompt, and move lasting facts into long-term

82
00:05:25,657 --> 00:05:26,183
memory.

83
00:05:26,533 --> 00:05:32,560
Summarize only when you pass a token
threshold, and drop old turns only after the

84
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:33,825
summary succeeds.

85
00:05:34,175 --> 00:05:36,712
The trap: summarizing on every turn.

86
00:05:37,062 --> 00:05:40,903
That adds a call each time,
and the summary slowly drifts.

87
00:05:41,503 --> 00:05:43,496
And: what can go wrong with summaries?

88
00:05:46,096 --> 00:05:49,554
Details get lost,
like exact numbers or code.

89
00:05:49,904 --> 00:05:51,914
Summaries of summaries can drift.

90
00:05:52,264 --> 00:05:54,672
And the summary call costs money and time.

91
00:05:55,022 --> 00:06:00,226
You reduce that with a clear list of
what to keep, a length limit, and long-term

92
00:06:00,226 --> 00:06:02,829
facts for anything that must stay exact.

93
00:06:03,179 --> 00:06:06,497
The trap is treating a
summary as a perfect record.

94
00:06:08,297 --> 00:06:09,156
Quick check.

95
00:06:09,506 --> 00:06:15,000
Why does compact drop the old
messages only after the summary call returns?

96
00:06:21,200 --> 00:06:24,882
Because if the call
fails, no conversation is lost.

97
00:06:26,682 --> 00:06:27,771
Let's recap.

98
00:06:28,121 --> 00:06:31,832
Summary memory turns old
turns into a running summary.

99
00:06:32,432 --> 00:06:34,643
Recent turns stay word for word.

100
00:06:35,243 --> 00:06:39,268
It costs one extra call,
only when you pass a token limit.

101
00:06:39,868 --> 00:06:44,229
Summarize first, then drop, and
tell the summarizer what to keep.

102
00:06:44,829 --> 00:06:47,051
Next lesson: long-term memory.

103
00:06:47,401 --> 00:06:53,017
Facts that survive a restart and a new
day, saved by the model with tools, and

104
00:06:53,017 --> 00:06:54,673
protected by your code.
